ce dc so that means  that the the difference of the two  operator is a  is a positive operator um  so so it is a generalization a  quantization of the  of the classical notion of local  testability  so what is known um about this notion  well there is a construction from 2017  from matthew hastings that gives  a soundness which is almost constant  it's one over log  cube of the length of the code and it  encodes  two logical qubits and in this works we  get a soundness which is a little better  it's a little smaller  it's um it's only one over  sorry it's a little larger it's only one  over  log squared of the length of the quantum  code but it encodes only one logical  qubit  and we have a conjecture that it's  possible to to also have a sun nest that  is one over  polylog with with a number of logical  qubits which is a great written one  which is uh greater than which is  non-constant sorry  so um to understand what locally  testable codes are  it's actually easier to picture some  codes  some quantum codes that are not locally  testable  and the most famous of them are not  for instance all the 2d codes like 2d  story codes if you take a chain  of errors that are all on the that are  on  edges of the toy code that are all  incidents that are pairwise incident to  each other  you will get a syndrome of weight only  two  and it's possible to do this for errors  of weights  there are of order square roots of n  square root of the  length of the code and and this shows  that  such codes are are not locally disabled  because to be locally testable you would  need to have a syndrome weight  that is linear in the size of the error  and for d-dimensional codes  d-dimensional theory codes the situation  is a little better  but it's also not locally testable to  see this you can think  of a four-dimensional three code for  instance where qubits are identified  with the two faces with packets  and if you think of an error that is  made of  of all this all the the two faces  in a big square well the number of  qubits  will be l squared where  l is the is the length of a side of the  square  and the perimeter of this square is  going to be 4l  so so you can see that the weight of the  syndrome is actually square root  of the weight of the air itself so it's  it's too small  to be locally testable and finally if  you  consider quantum expander codes as  i said before they have some they  satisfy  the locality stability inequality but  only for errors  that are smaller that have the weights  more than  square root of the length of the code so  for some errors  that that have weight of the order of  the square root of the length of the  code  um you will have a constant energy  and that that shows that quantum  expander codes are none  are not locally testable codes  so now i will give the the construction  of the hemi cubic code the one that  gives the  this good local disability and let me  mention that this construction  was previously called the projective  code in a previous conference  so um such codes  give you the following nkd parameters k  is always equal to one but the minimum  distance  grows like the square root of the  number of physical qubits so this is  similar to  what you have for surface codes  but the big difference is that um you do  have a good soundness namely 1  over log squared of the number of  physical qubits  and we also have a generalization that  encodes a polynomial number of logical  qubits  and still has a minimum distance which  is polynomial  in the number of physical qubits but for  this generalization  the local physics testability is only  conjectured  so what's the idea between  behind the construction it's it starts  with a  well-known fact about two-dimensional  codes  namely a connection between the geometry  of two-dimensional surfaces and the  minimum distance  of codes built from tessellations of  such  surfaces the number of physical qubits  will be  proportional to the area of such a  surface  and the minimum distance will be  proportional to the  shortest closed loop on such a surface  um so with this intuition in mind if we  want to maximize the minimum distance  at a fixed um  at a fixed area it's the same as  minimizing  the area at the fixed system at a fixed  minimum distance  so it makes sense to do this to consider  some surface with a positive curvature  because the the discs  in the surface with positive curators  have an  area which is smaller for a given radius  of such a disc so it's a good  direction to look into and and indeed  if we consider the real projective plane  so what's a real projective plane it's  it's a sphere such that antipodal points  are identified  well such such a 2d surface  has a systel equal to pi if we consider  a sphere with a radius one but its  area is only two pi so  it shows that the the systole in this  case is greater than the square root of  the area  and this would be a hint that the  minimum distance would be greater than  square root of n  but it's not possible to construct an  infinite family of quantum codes  with this construction so we'll need to  do something else then  only real projective plane will consider  real projective  spaces in them in dimension more than  two  and this is similar to construction by  hastings in 2016  where here we considered high  dimensional manifolds  so instead of doing it in a geometric  setting we will directly go to a  discrete setting  and take cubes this way  we have an easy discretization  of of the geometry and to turn  so cube is is topologically uh like a  sphere if you take the  the the outer surface of this cube and  to make it  uh have the same topology as a  projective plane  we will identify opposite antipodal  edges  and typical vertices and antipotential  two phases  and by doing so we get a quantum code  that has one minimum distance equal to  three  as you can see the the loop in red  has length six but since opposites edges  are identified  it actually has lens three and  the other minimum distance is two  um the one where you take  um co boundaries of edges and  it's interesting to note that the  products of these two minimum distances  is equal to the number of physical  qubits  of this construction and indeed this  generalizes to hypercubes of dimension  more than three if you identify qubits  with p  faces for any p between one and n minus  one  you will um it's it's possible to  compute the minimum distances  of such a code and uh it will always  hold  that the number of physical qubits is  the product of the two minimum distances  so it's not possible to to break the  square root of n barrier with such a  construction but it's possible to reach  it  at least um  and um in terms of local testability  well we have to normalize  well since since the hemi cubic code  has weights check weights  that is logarithmic in the number of  physical qubits  we have to take this into account and  normalize the soundness  the constant of the locality stability  and we can prove that we will get a  soundness which is 1 over log  squared of the number of physical qubits  so since  the constant of the soundness is  the constant between a syndrome that is  a boundary  and the air corresponding to this  syndrome that is a feeling of this  boundary  what we are looking at for the hypercube  is  filling inequalities and luckily  um this is a subject that has already  been looked at  notably by doterra in 2012  and he noted that it's possible to find  filling inequalities of um  of of small  where the filling has a small weight  compared to the size of the boundary  and it's actually an algorith an  algorithmic  proof a constructive proof where the  feeling is given  by fixing a direction and  and sending this way all the syndrome  to one side of the hypercube  and then iterating by choosing other  directions of the hypercube  and by doing that as you can see  on the on the drawing on the right it's  possible to  have a boundary 